From f7446937c314e366006489c1aa55a917b64d2823 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NathanNeurotic <109461996+NathanNeurotic@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 06:52:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] smbman: a write is complete only for the bytes the server
 says it wrote

smb_WriteAndX() returned the requested byte count for every reply with a
success status, without reading the response's Count field. MS-CIFS
2.2.4.43.2 lets a server answer a write to a full disk with
STATUS_SUCCESS and a Count of zero (the EFBIG and NOSPC rows of its
error table), and a server may store fewer bytes than it was sent and
say so in Count. Against such a server a write cut short was reported to
the caller as complete.

Return the server's Count instead. smb_WriteFile() already advances by
the returned value, so a short Count now sends the rest from the right
buffer position and file offset. A zero Count or a failed request ends
the write. smb_WriteFile() then returns the bytes that earlier requests
of the same call stored, so the caller's file position matches what the
server holds; when there were none it returns 0 or the error, as
before. A reply shorter than the six-word WRITE_ANDX response, a
WordCount under 6, or a Count larger than the request is a failed write
(-EIO). CountHigh is not read: smb_WriteFile() never asks for more than
CLIENT_MAX_XFER_SIZE (65535) bytes in one request.

Servers that report a full disk with an error status, as Samba does,
still fail the write with -EIO; a call whose earlier requests stored
data now reports those bytes first, and the next call gets the error.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01RwNwRWSWvoz4YfK5AEj37D
---
 iop/network/smbman/src/smb.c | 16 +++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/iop/network/smbman/src/smb.c b/iop/network/smbman/src/smb.c
index 4d201bd98..efb3ae306 100644
--- a/iop/network/smbman/src/smb.c
+++ b/iop/network/smbman/src/smb.c
@@ -1119,7 +1119,17 @@ int smb_WriteAndX(int UID, int TID, int FID, s64 fileoffset, void *writebuf, int
     if ((WRsp->smbH.Eclass | (WRsp->smbH.Ecode << 16)) != STATUS_SUCCESS)
         return -EIO;
 
-    return nbytes;
+    // A success status does not mean every byte was written. MS-CIFS 2.2.4.43.2 lets a server
+    // report a full disk with an error status (caught above) or with success and a Count of zero,
+    // and a server may store less than it was sent. So return the server's Count: smb_WriteFile()
+    // sends the rest from there, and on a zero or a failure returns what earlier requests stored.
+    // It asks for at most CLIENT_MAX_XFER_SIZE (65535) bytes, so the 16-bit Count holds the whole
+    // answer and CountHigh is not read. A reply shorter than the six-word response (45 bytes), a
+    // WordCount under 6, or a Count over what was sent is a failed write.
+    if (r < (int)sizeof(WriteAndXResponse_t) || WRsp->smbWordcount < 6 || WRsp->Count > nbytes)
+        return -EIO;
+
+    return WRsp->Count;
 }
 
 int smb_WriteFile(int UID, int TID, int FID, s64 fileoffset, void *writebuf, int nbytes)
@@ -1138,8 +1148,8 @@ int smb_WriteFile(int UID, int TID, int FID, s64 fileoffset, void *writebuf, int
         toWrite = remaining > CLIENT_MAX_XFER_SIZE ? CLIENT_MAX_XFER_SIZE : remaining;
 
         result = smb_WriteAndX(UID, TID, FID, pos, ptr, toWrite);
-        if (result <= 0)
-            return result;
+        if (result <= 0) // the server stored what earlier requests sent, and no more
+            return remaining < nbytes ? nbytes - remaining : result;
 
         pos += result;
         ptr += result;
-- 
